## Claim
Text: "WHO ( World Health Organization) approved water, salt and vinegar remedy for coronavirus"
Claim author: Shanika.live
Claim date: October 08, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Has the World Health Organization (WHO) ever issued an official statement or guideline approving or recommending a combination of water, salt, and vinegar as a treatment or preventive measure for COVID-19?
Answer: No, the World Health Organization has not issued any official statement or guideline approving or recommending a combination of water, salt, and vinegar as a treatment or preventive measure for COVID-19. As cited in the source, the WHO explicitly notes that "to date, there is no specific medicine recommended to prevent or treat the new coronavirus."

Source URL: https://www.factcheck.org/2020/03/gargling-water-with-salt-wont-eliminate-coronavirus/

### What specific date or period did the World Health Organization (WHO) announce the approval of a water, salt, and vinegar remedy for the coronavirus?
Answer: The World Health Organization (WHO) did not approve water, salt, and vinegar as a remedy for the coronavirus at any time; the claim is false. According to The Quint's fact-check, the assertion that such a treatment is WHO-approved is part of a disinformation campaign, and medical experts have clarified that "there’s no medical evidence" to support its efficacy against COVID-19.

Source URL: https://www.thequint.com/news/webqoof/hot-water-gargles-cure-covid-19-claim-not-medically-corroborated

### Where can the official WHO document, webpage, or video announcement approving this specific water, salt, and vinegar remedy be found?
Answer: The official WHO resource would be their "Coronavirus disease (COVID-19) advice for the public: Myth busters" page, which the cited FactCheck.org article references. That WHO page explicitly states there is "no specific medicine recommended to prevent or treat the new coronavirus," thus it does not approve the water, salt, and vinegar remedy.

Source URL: https://www.factcheck.org/2020/03/gargling-water-with-salt-wont-eliminate-coronavirus/

### Is there any clinical evidence or published research endorsed by the WHO showing that a water, salt, and vinegar mixture is effective against SARS-CoV-2, the virus causing COVID-19?
Answer: No, there is no clinical evidence or WHO endorsement for a water, salt, and vinegar mixture against SARS-CoV-2. The World Health Organization states that "to date, there is no specific medicine recommended to prevent or treat the new coronavirus," and experts confirm that while saline gargles may soothe a sore throat, "it doesn’t kill the virus."

Source URL: https://www.factcheck.org/2020/03/gargling-water-with-salt-wont-eliminate-coronavirus/

### Does the World Health Organization (WHO) have a known list of approved or recommended treatments for COVID-19, and does a water, salt, and vinegar remedy appear on that list?
Answer: Yes, the WHO maintains a "living guideline" on drugs for COVID-19, which includes recommendations for and against specific treatments. The guideline recommends pharmaceutical treatments such as nirmatrelvir/ritonavir, remdesivir, molnupiravir (for non-severe illness in high-risk patients), systemic corticosteroids, IL-6 receptor blockers, and baricitinib (for severe or critical illness). It also explicitly recommends against various treatments including ivermectin, fluvoxamine, colchicine, convalescent plasma, and certain monoclonal antibodies. A water, salt, and vinegar remedy does not appear anywhere in the WHO's recommended treatments for COVID-19.

Source URL: https://www.bmj.com/content/370/bmj.m3379

### What is the exact composition and recommended use (e.g., gargling, drinking, cleaning) of the water, salt, and vinegar remedy that was allegedly approved by the WHO?
Answer: The false claim described a remedy of gargling with lukewarm water mixed with salt or vinegar, but the search result confirms this is not approved by the WHO and has been debunked by health authorities, who state "there is no medical evidence that rinsing the throat with warm water and salt or vinegar will eliminate the virus that causes COVID-19."

Source URL: https://verafiles.org/articles/vera-files-fact-check-gargling-water-salt-vinegar-does-not-r

